update public.curriculum_units as c set description = v.description from (values
('0aaf3128-0c0d-40da-9ea1-09151edf5aee'::uuid, 'Light supplies the energy that drives photosynthesis. In this lesson, you will explain how light intensity affects the rate of photosynthesis, interpret the general relationship between light availability and food production, and understand why the rate eventually becomes limited by another factor.'),
('33faf322-c71c-42d7-a733-c4fb814982ec'::uuid, 'Carbon dioxide is one of the raw materials used during photosynthesis. This section examines how its concentration affects the rate of the process and why increasing its availability can raise the rate only while other necessary conditions remain suitable.'),
('358d38fe-9b37-4c06-8123-883d6ae2ca18'::uuid, 'Photosynthesis supplies plants with organic food, but successful growth also depends on mineral elements obtained from the soil. This section examines the nutrients plants require, the roles they play and the effects of deficiencies. You will connect mineral nutrition with the formation of healthy tissues and the proper functioning of plant processes.'),
('3634d333-58a9-4507-95aa-dbf0a80797d2'::uuid, 'Photosynthesis is controlled by enzyme-mediated reactions, so temperature influences how quickly the process can occur. This section examines how changes in temperature affect photosynthetic activity and why very low or very high temperatures can reduce the rate.'),
('3fab5349-2cda-4582-ac37-0ef3baaca4dd'::uuid, 'Plants obtain some of the materials needed for growth from the soil, even though they manufacture their own organic food. This theme focuses on mineral nutrients, why plants need them and how a shortage of particular elements affects growth and development.'),
('3ff0e348-9a2d-4f60-8424-d91906e51cf3'::uuid, 'The rate of photosynthesis changes when environmental conditions change. In this lesson, you will introduce light intensity, carbon dioxide concentration and temperature as factors affecting the process. You will explain the idea of a limiting factor and prepare to examine how each condition influences photosynthesis.'),
('6493ef0d-9651-4968-bd80-29b9b1aa7078'::uuid, 'Plants need a reliable supply of materials to build new tissues and maintain their activities. This section introduces plant nutrition, distinguishing the organic food plants manufacture from the mineral elements they absorb from the soil. It prepares you to examine photosynthesis and mineral nutrition, and to understand how both contribute to healthy plant growth.'),
('80b4d481-40a4-4937-8e79-6c835d431dd4'::uuid, 'Temperature affects the enzyme-controlled reactions involved in photosynthesis. In this lesson, you will describe how the rate changes as temperature rises towards a suitable optimum and explain why excessive temperatures can reduce photosynthetic activity. You will relate the pattern to the effects of temperature on enzymes.'),
('8d27a8a0-f340-43cf-86ef-b8d635160b63'::uuid, 'Carbon dioxide is required for the production of carbohydrates during photosynthesis. In this lesson, you will explain how carbon dioxide concentration affects the rate of the process and interpret why the rate may stop increasing when another factor, such as light or temperature, becomes limiting.'),
('97f7fd08-1714-42e6-a37f-eddd34a0008d'::uuid, 'Having established that plants manufacture organic food, now examine the process that makes this possible. This section explores photosynthesis, including the raw materials, conditions and structures involved. You will learn how light energy is used to make carbohydrates and how the process supports the growth and functioning of plants.'),
('a488f79a-f402-4776-a384-0afe40e4b8dd'::uuid, 'Plants require mineral elements in suitable amounts for normal growth and development. In this lesson, you will identify important mineral nutrients, explain their functions and recognise symptoms that may appear when particular elements are lacking. You will understand why healthy plant growth depends on both soil nutrients and photosynthesis.'),
('a5079d6a-594d-46e7-b2bc-4baed36ff35f'::uuid, 'Light provides the energy needed for photosynthesis. This section narrows the study of limiting factors to light intensity, explaining how the amount of light reaching a leaf can influence the rate of food production and why the response may level off when another factor becomes limiting.'),
('a6e3227e-0120-44ba-9657-523eb9a830b9'::uuid, 'Plants make organic food, but they also depend on mineral elements absorbed from the soil. In this lesson, you will distinguish photosynthetic nutrition from mineral nutrition and explain why plants need both manufactured carbohydrates and inorganic nutrients to grow and maintain their tissues.'),
('d25cdb08-4d95-4d43-a9e6-89d289b140e9'::uuid, 'The rate of photosynthesis does not remain constant under all conditions. This section examines how light intensity, carbon dioxide concentration and temperature influence the process. You will learn why each factor can limit photosynthesis and how changing one condition can affect the rate at which a plant produces food.'),
('d9b309d2-f82e-47da-ac50-bf2f231c17fd'::uuid, 'Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy stored in organic substances. In this lesson, you will describe the process, identify the raw materials and products, and explain the roles of chlorophyll and light. You will connect the process with the production of food that supports plant growth and provides energy-rich materials for other organisms.'),
('e7f86fdb-2099-48fd-8b87-0c67bc9ca15f'::uuid, 'The need for food in plants leads directly to photosynthesis, the process by which light energy supports the production of organic substances. This theme introduces the overall process and the conditions it requires, preparing you to examine the materials used and the factors that influence its rate.'),
('ea94eee2-45b8-4fb9-b3d3-9752bd9caa62'::uuid, 'With the role of photosynthesis established, focus on how the process takes place. This theme brings together the need for light, chlorophyll, water and carbon dioxide, and prepares you to explain how these inputs are used to form carbohydrates.'),
('f8e7180a-b31f-4a43-a58a-7175f1690a40'::uuid, 'The process of photosynthesis depends not only on the plant''s structures and raw materials but also on the surrounding conditions. This theme introduces the main limiting factors and prepares you to consider the separate effects of light intensity, carbon dioxide concentration and temperature.')
) as v(id,description) where c.id=v.id and c.description is distinct from v.description;
